  "title": "CHAPTER 9: HISTORY OF RAJASTHAN – MEDIEVAL PERIOD",
  "order": 10,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "18ic99w",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "9.1 Rajput Kingdoms (6th–16th Century)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The medieval period was dominated by powerful Rajput kingdoms, characterized by warfare, chivalry, culture, and architecture. The major kingdoms were:",
      "id": "311uu89"
    },
    {
      "id": "towrtfy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "9.1.1 Mewar (Udaipur)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Mewar was the most prestigious and powerful Rajput kingdom. The Sisodia dynasty ruled Mewar. The founder of the Guhilot dynasty was Guhil (6th century). Bappa Rawal (8th century) is considered the real founder of Mewar kingdom and took Chittorgarh from the Mauryas. He defeated Arab invaders.",
      "id": "tsk7ere"
    },
    {
      "id": "ynaxetj",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Ruler</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Period &amp; Achievement</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Bappa Rawal</p>",
            "<p>8th century; Founder; defeated Arabs</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Jaitrasingh</p>",
            "<p>Defeated Shamsuddin Iltutmish (Delhi Sultanate)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Hammir Singh</p>",
            "<p>Re-established Mewar after Alauddin's conquest</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Rana Kumbha</p>",
            "<p>1433-1468; Built Vijay Stambha, Kumbhalgarh Fort</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Rana Sanga (Sangram Singh)</p>",
            "<p>1508-1528; Led coalition against Babur at First Battle of Panipat</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Rana Udai Singh</p>",
            "<p>Founded Udaipur in 1559</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Maharana Pratap</p>",
            "<p>1572-1597; Battle of Haldighati (1576)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Rana Raj Singh</p>",
            "<p>Stopped Aurangzeb's Jizya; helped Marwar</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "5a2o1bs",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "9.1.2 Marwar (Jodhpur)"
    },
    {
      "id": "0jxz44y",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "yc279jz",
      "src": "/books/Guide/RAJASTHAN SI GS GUIDE/images/image_yc279jz.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The Rathore dynasty ruled Marwar. Rao Siha (1273) founded the Rathore dynasty at Pali. Rao Chunda (early 15th century) captured Mandore and made it capital. Rao Jodha (1459) founded Jodhpur and built Mehrangarh Fort.",
      "id": "qz6c45u"
    },
    {
      "id": "aribmco",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Ruler</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Achievement</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Rao Jodha</p>",
            "<p>Founded Jodhpur city (1459); built Mehrangarh Fort</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Maldeo</p>",
            "<p>Most powerful ruler; resisted Sher Shah Suri</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Chandrasen</p>",
            "<p>Known as 'Forgotten Hero'; fought Akbar</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Raja Jaswant Singh</p>",
            "<p>Fought Aurangzeb</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Maharaja Abhay Singh</p>",
            "<p>Defeated Gujarat Subah</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ydj6542",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "9.1.3 Amber/Jaipur (Kachwaha Dynasty)"
    },
    {
      "id": "fvxyzdd",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Ruler</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Achievement</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Man Singh I</p>",
            "<p>Akbar's general; built Man Sagar Dam</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Mirza Raja Jai Singh I</p>",
            "<p>Military commander under Shah Jahan &amp; Aurangzeb</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Sawai Jai Singh II</p>",
            "<p>Founded Jaipur in 1727; built Jantar Mantar observatories (5 cities)</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "xvmt9ia",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "9.1.4 Bikaner"
    },
    {
      "id": "05w4ppn",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "z5ak7o9",
      "src": "/books/Guide/RAJASTHAN SI GS GUIDE/images/image_z5ak7o9.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Founded by Rao Bika in 1488, a son of Rao Jodha of Marwar. Junagarh Fort was built by Rai Singh (1589-1594). Maharaja Ganga Singh modernised Bikaner and built Gang Canal.",
      "id": "7pffhb3"
    },
    {
      "id": "81d06g4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "9.1.5 Jaisalmer"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Founded by Rawal Jaisal (Bhati clan) in 1156 AD. The Sonar Quila (Golden Fort) of Jaisalmer is a UNESCO World Heritage Site (along with 5 other Rajasthan forts). Three Saka (mass self-immolations) occurred in Jaisalmer Fort.",
      "id": "hhbb6d3"
    },
    {
